/documents/s15467/7a-cap-inv-strategy-appendix-1-corporate-investment-strategy.pdf","committee":"Council","date":"05/03/2026","key":true},{"title":"SOA 3 - Statement of Accounts 2024-25 Report","url":"https://democracy.derbyshiredales.gov.uk/documents/s15122/soa-3-statement-of-accounts-2024-25-report.pdf","committee":"Governance & Resources Committee","date":"22/01/2026","key":true},{"title":"SOA 3 - Appendix 2 - Statement of Accounts 2024-25","url":"https://democracy.derbyshiredales.gov.uk/documents/s15123/soa-3-appendix-2-statement-of-accounts-2024-25.pdf","committee":"Governance & Resources Committee","date":"22/01/2026","key":true},{"title":"9c. Capital Prudential Indicators","url":"https://democracy.derbyshiredales.gov.uk/documents/s14825/9c-capital-prudential-indicators.pdf","committee":"Council","date":"27/11/2025","key":true},{"title":"2. Report - Consultation and Equality Action Plans 2024-26 - Outturn 2024-25","url":"https://democracy.derbyshiredales.gov.uk/documents/s14829/2-report-consultation-and-equality-action-plans-2024-26-outt.pdf","committee":"Council","date":"27/11/2025","key":true},{"title":"2a. Appendix 1 - Consultation & Engagement Action Plan Outturn 2024-25","url":"https://democracy.derbyshiredales.gov.uk/documents/s14830/2a-appendix-1-consultation-engagement-action-plan-outturn-20.pdf","committee":"Council","date":"27/11/2025","key":true}],"read_documents":[],"calendar":{"upcoming":[{"date":"2026-09-24","committee":"Council","time":"6.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.derbyshiredales.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-11-05","committee":"Governance & Resources Committee","time":"6.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.derbyshiredales.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-11-26","committee":"Council","time":"6.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.derbyshiredales.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-12-03","committee":"Council","time":"6.00 pm","status":"Confirmed; Extraordinary Local Plan","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.derbyshiredales.gov.uk"}],"recent":[{"date":"2026-06-18","committee":"Council","time":"6.00 pm","status":"Confirmed; Extraordinary","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.derbyshiredales.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-07-02","committee":"Governance & Resources Committee","time":"6.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.derbyshiredales.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-07-22","committee":"Council","time":"6.07 pm","status":"Confirmed; Extraordinary","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.derbyshiredales.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-07-23","committee":"Council","time":"6.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.derbyshiredales.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-03","committee":"Governance & Resources Committee","time":"6.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.derbyshiredales.gov.uk"}]},"news":[],"news_drafts":[{"headline":"Derbyshire Dales faces \u00a34.2m budget gap by 2030 as capital reserves drain and underlying spending pressures mount.","lead":"Derbyshire Dales Council is heading toward a structural budget deficit of nearly \u00a31m in 2027\u201328, rising to \u00a34.2m by 2029\u201330, according to its latest financial monitoring report to the council meeting on 24 September. The council's usable capital reserves, money set aside for building and equipment, will shrink from \u00a315.1m to \u00a35.9m over five years, leaving only \u00a3331k available for new schemes. Officials say borrowing to fund capital projects is unlikely to be affordable given the council's medium-term financial position. The council is already using prior-year surpluses and drawing down its general reserves\u2014which will fall from \u00a327.3m to \u00a312.3m by March 2028\u2014to plug annual budget gaps. A Q1 underspend of \u00a3376k masks weaker underlying finances: it is driven almost entirely by one-off items including \u00a3281k extra investment income and \u00a3200k from a single planning application. The council faces recurring staffing cost pressures, including a \u00a3119k overspend this year, and has flagged financial resilience as high risk on its strategic risk register pending delivery of unidentified savings.","iso":"2026-09-24","paper_title":"Capital Programme Update Quarter 1 Sept Council 2026","paper_url":"https://derbyshiredales.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s17126/17126.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"Derbyshire Dales faces \u00a3990k budget gap with no savings plan","lead":"Derbyshire Dales council faces a budget shortfall of nearly \u00a31m in 2027/28 that its own forecasts show will widen to \u00a34.2m by 2029/30. The council has not identified any savings to close this gap. It is covering annual shortfalls by drawing down its reserves, money it has set aside, which are forecast to fall from \u00a327.3m to \u00a312.3m by the end of 2028/29, and from one-off sources such as investment income. The council has also stated that borrowing for future capital investment, money spent on long-lived assets such as buildings, is unlikely to be affordable given its financial position. Its capital reserves are forecast to fall from \u00a315.1m to \u00a35.9m over five years.","iso":"2026-09-24","paper_title":"Capital Programme Update Quarter 1 Sept Council 2026","paper_url":"https://derbyshiredales.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s17126/17126.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true}],"findings":[{"iso":"2026-09-24","committee":"Council","paper_title":"Revenue Budget Monitoring Quarter 1 202627","paper_url":"https://derbyshiredales.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s17127/17127.pdf","headline":"Council is incurring kennelling costs exceeding budget (\u00a353k overspend) linked to an 'ongoing legal matter' with no detail provided in the report.","detail":"The paper discloses a \u00a353k overspend on kennelling but offers only that 'this is an ongoing legal matter' with 'a report will be presented to Members in due course'. No detail on the nature, duration, or estimated resolution cost of this matter is provided, creating transparency gap.","category":"other","matters":"Unquantified legal liabilities are a concern for lenders; a legal matter sufficient to cause \u00a353k quarterly overspend may represent a larger hidden exposure or signal governance/compliance issues.","figures":"Kennelling budget pressure: \u00a353k (forecast annual overspend, implies \u00a313.25k per quarter)","quote":"In response to an ongoing situation, the Council is incurring kennelling costs that exceed the current budget. This is an ongoing legal matter, a report will be presented to Members in due course.","material":false},{"iso":"2026-09-24","committee":"Council","paper_title":"Revenue Budget Monitoring Quarter 1 202627","paper_url":"https://derbyshiredales.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s17127/17127.pdf","headline":"Local government reorganisation uncertainty is explicitly flagged as having 'important implications' for financial strategy; the council will defer reserve-allocation decisions pending clarity on LGR costs and structure.","detail":"The council notes that LGR uncertainty has 'important implications for the Council's financial strategy and medium-term financial resilience' and specifically recommends deferring any decision on allocating the Q1 underspend 'pending the delivery of the required savings and the outcome of the Local Government Finance Settlement for 2027/28 and beyond' and 'anticipated one-off LGR transition costs'. This suggests LGR is a material financial risk factor.","category":"governance","matters":"A council facing reorganisation/merger is higher financial risk: it cannot commit to multi-year savings plans, will face one-off transition costs, and may lose economies of scale or absorb a weaker authority's debts.","figures":"Not quantified","quote":"The uncertainty surrounding Local Government Reorganisation (LGR) has important implications for the Council's financial strategy and medium-term financial resilience.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-24","committee":"Council","paper_title":"Revenue Budget Monitoring Quarter 1 202627","paper_url":"https://derbyshiredales.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s17127/17127.pdf","headline":"Council faces organisational resilience issues and is allocating \u00a350k from the forecast underspend to the recruitment and retention reserve to address them.","detail":"The paper allocates \u00a350k of the Q1 underspend to recruitment and retention reserve 'to ensure that there is an adequate balance available...to fund the proposals to address organisational resilience issues'. These issues are the subject of a separate report to the same council meeting, indicating management has identified problems beyond routine staffing management.","category":"people","matters":"Organisational resilience issues\u2014typically HR, morale, retention, or capability problems\u2014create operational risk and can impair service delivery or drive unforeseen cost increases.","figures":"Reserve allocation for resilience: \u00a350k","quote":"\u00a350,000 be transferred to the recruitment and retention reserve to ensure that there is an adequate balance available in the reserve to fund the proposals to address organisational resilience issues that are the subject of a report elsewhere on the agenda for this meeting.","material":false},{"iso":"2026-09-24","committee":"Council","paper_title":"Revenue Budget Monitoring Quarter 1 202627","paper_url":"https://derbyshiredales.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s17127/17127.pdf","headline":"Staffing costs overrunning by \u00a3119k due to turnover provision tripled from \u00a395k to \u00a3336k and pay award higher than budgeted.","detail":"The turnover provision was increased 'in response to recurring staffing underspends arising from vacant posts and staff turnover, and to bring the budget more in line with those of other Derbyshire authorities'. Pay award came in at 3.3% against a 3% budget. This indicates persistent staff retention problems and rising employment costs that are recurring, not one-off.","category":"overspend","matters":"Staffing is typically the largest cost item in local government; if retention costs are rising faster than income and require multiple years of catch-up provision, this signals structural cost pressure.","figures":"Staffing overspend 2026/27: \u00a3119k; Turnover provision increased from \u00a395k to \u00a3336k; Pay award: 3.3% vs 3% budgeted","quote":"Staffing budgets are forecast to overspend by \u00a3119k. This is mainly due to an increased staff turnover provision included in the 2026/27 budget, which rose from \u00a395k to \u00a3336k, and the impact of the 2026/27 pay award, which was 3.3% rather than the budgeted 3.0%.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-24","committee":"Council","paper_title":"Revenue Budget Monitoring Quarter 1 202627","paper_url":"https://derbyshiredales.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s17127/17127.pdf","headline":"Q1 underspend of \u00a3376k is driven by two one-off income items that will not recur: \u00a3281k investment income upside and \u00a3200k planning fees from a single major application.","detail":"The apparent underspend masks a weaker underlying position. Investment income outperformance (\u00a3281k) stems from interest rates holding at 3.75% rather than the budgeted assumption of 3.5% by April\u2014this is contingent on rates and non-recurring. Planning income (\u00a3200k surplus) is explicitly labelled a one-off, 'largely attributable to a single major application, which generated planning fees of \u00a3258k'. Together these account for \u00a3481k of the \u00a3376k reported underspend.","category":"income","matters":"A lender interpreting the Q1 underspend as evidence of good budget management would be misled; the underlying run-rate position is weaker because these income sources are not structural.","figures":"Q1 underspend: \u00a3376.418k; Investment income outperformance: \u00a3281k; Planning income one-off: \u00a3200k (from single application)","quote":"the \u00a3200k forecast overachievement is considered a one-off benefit. This is largely attributable to a single major application, which generated planning fees of \u00a3258k.","material":true}],"editorial":null,"freshness":{"last_paper_iso":"2026-09-24"},"name":"Derbyshire Dales","daily_post":{"iso":"2026-09-24","since_iso":"2026-08-25","distress":false,"headline":"Council is incurring kennelling costs exceeding budget (\u00a353k overspend) linked to an 'ongoing legal matter' with no detail provided in the report.","lead":"Unquantified legal liabilities are a concern for lenders; a legal matter sufficient to cause \u00a353k quarterly overspend may represent a larger hidden exposure or signal governance/compliance issues.","also":["12 other findings in this window"],"watch":"Governance & Resources Committee on 2026-11-05","n_findings":13,"n_documents":0,"top_paper_title":"Revenue Budget Monitoring Quarter 1 202627","top_paper_url":"https://derbyshiredales.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s17127/17127.pdf","draft":true},"findings_total":76,"news_drafts_total":2}}